###  https://purl.org/fairops/indiv#PredictiveParity
indiv:PredictiveParity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                core:ObservationalFairnessNotion ,
                                core:SufficiencyFairnessNotion ;
                       core:extends indiv:Calibration ;
                       core:isProposedBy <https://purl.org/fairops/papers#2016_DIETERICH> ,
                                         <https://purl.org/fairops/papers#2017_CHOULDECHOVA> ,
                                         <https://purl.org/fairops/papers#2017_KUSNER> ;
                       core:isSuitableFor core:Conversation ,
                                          core:Prediction ;
                       core:isUsedBy <https://purl.org/fairops/papers#2020_MANSOURY> ;
                       core:refersTo core:Classification ,
                                     core:TextGeneration ;
                       rdfs:comment "2020_MANSOURY use precision (predictive parity) as a benchmarck metric to evaluate the fraction of the recommended items shown to the users that are part of the users’ profile in the test set. While predictive parity and calibration look like very similar criteria, test fair scores can fail to satisfy predictive parity at a given threshold. This is because the relationship between predictive parity and test fairness depends on the conditional distribution of R | A , which can differ across groups in ways that result in PPV imbalance. In the simple case where R itself is binary (Y^), a score that is test fair will also satisfy predictive parity." .


###  https://purl.org/fairops/indiv#PrejudiceIndex
indiv:PrejudiceIndex rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                     core:isProposedBy <https://purl.org/fairops/papers#2012_KAMISHIMA> ;
                     core:isSuitableFor core:Prediction ;
                     core:measures indiv:CounterfactualFairness ;
                     core:refersTo core:Classification ;
                     core:scientificArtifactDescription """An index to quantify the degree of indirect prejudice is defined as the mutual information between $Y$ (target random variable to be predicted) and $S$ (sensitive variable), considering the empirical (sample) distributions over a given dataset $\\mathcal{D}$:
\\[
\\mathrm{PI} = \\sum_{(y,s)\\in \\mathcal{D}} \\hat{P}(y,s)\\,\\ln \\frac{\\hat{P}(y,s)}{\\hat{P}(y)\\,\\hat{P}(s)}.
\\]""" .


